  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25e-perchlike-fishes-5-remaining-cichlids-from-south-and-central-america/caquetaia_myersi_en/",
      "html": "<p>Caquetaia myersi is a real rarity in the aquaria of the world. It grows up to 25 cm long. Origin: bred (German bred specimens).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25e-perchlike-fishes-5-remaining-cichlids-from-south-and-central-america/caquetaia_myersi_en/",
      "html": "<p>It occurs in the wild in Colombia, far from the usual collecting sites. Fishing in those areas is made even more difficult by the presence of guerrillas, who have quite different interests than keeping and breeding ornamental fish.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25e-perchlike-fishes-5-remaining-cichlids-from-south-and-central-america/caquetaia_myersi_en/",
      "html": "<p>Due to the size the species can reach, larger tanks (500 litres upwards) are recommended. However, it is also possible to keep and breed a pair in much smaller tanks, as Caquetaia are predators and do not swim more than necessary.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25e-perchlike-fishes-5-remaining-cichlids-from-south-and-central-america/caquetaia_myersi_en/",
      "html": "<p>Caquetaia prey on their victims with a strike. Food has to be chosen according to the size of the fish. Juveniles can be fed flakes and frozen food, but adults need frozen smelt or cichlid sticks to thrive well.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25e-perchlike-fishes-5-remaining-cichlids-from-south-and-central-america/caquetaia_myersi_en/",
      "html": "<p>C. myersi can be kept with other fish better than its close relatives, C. kraussii and C. spectabilis. Even fully grown adults can be kept with other large species of cichlid, tetra, or catfish. Those tankmates do not have to fear anything from C. myersi. Even when a pair leads up to 600 young, it is able to defend them without hurting any other fish.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25e-perchlike-fishes-5-remaining-cichlids-from-south-and-central-america/caquetaia_myersi_en/",
      "html": "<p>Etymology: Caquetaia is named after the Rio Caquetá in Colombia. myersi is a dedication name.</p>"
    }
  ],
